## Exercise 6 — `is not null` versus `!= null`

Edit `src/Lessons/V3CA02_PatternMatching/Ex6.cs`. `Sneaky` is written for you
and it lies:

```csharp
public sealed class Sneaky
{
    public static bool operator ==(Sneaky? left, Sneaky? right) => false;
    public static bool operator !=(Sneaky? left, Sneaky? right) => true;
    public override bool Equals(object? obj) => false;
    public override int GetHashCode() => 0;
}
```

Implement:

```csharp
public static class NullSafe
{
    public static bool IsMissing<T>(T? value) where T : class;
    public static int CountMissing(IEnumerable<object?> values);
}
```

`IsMissing` returns `true` exactly when the reference is null — including for
`Sneaky`, whose operators claim otherwise. `CountMissing` counts the nulls in a
sequence that mixes real nulls, `Sneaky` instances and ordinary objects.

- `IsMissing<Sneaky>(null)` -> `true`
- `IsMissing(new Sneaky())` -> `false`
- `CountMissing([null, new Sneaky(), "x", null])` -> `2`

The gate additionally asserts that `new Sneaky() != null` evaluates to `true`
and that `new Sneaky() is not null` also evaluates to `true` — the two agree
here. It then asserts they disagree for a *null* `Sneaky` reference. Work out
which of the two is wrong before you look at the hint.

