## Exercise 7 — a recursive evaluator

Edit `src/Lessons/V3CA02_PatternMatching/Ex7.cs`.

```csharp
public abstract record Expr
{
    private Expr() { }

    public sealed record Num(int Value) : Expr;
    public sealed record Add(Expr Left, Expr Right) : Expr;
    public sealed record Mul(Expr Left, Expr Right) : Expr;
    public sealed record Neg(Expr Inner) : Expr;
}

public static class Evaluator
{
    public static int Eval(Expr expr);
    public static int EvalCount { get; }
}
```

`Eval` is **one** switch expression whose arms match on the structure of the
children, applying these simplifications before the general cases:

- `Mul(Num(0), _)` and `Mul(_, Num(0))` -> `0`, without evaluating the other side
- `Add(Num(0), var x)` and `Add(var x, Num(0))` -> `Eval(x)`
- `Mul(Num(1), var x)` and `Mul(var x, Num(1))` -> `Eval(x)`
- `Neg(Neg(var x))` -> `Eval(x)`
- then the general `Num`, `Add`, `Mul`, `Neg` cases

`EvalCount` counts how many times `Eval` has been entered since the last
`Reset()`. The gate builds `Mul(Num(0), <a deep tree>)` and asserts `EvalCount`
is `1` — proving the right subtree was never walked.

- `Eval(Add(Num(2), Num(3)))` -> `5`
- `Eval(Neg(Neg(Num(7))))` -> `7` in 2 evaluations, not 4
- `Eval(Mul(Num(1), Add(Num(2), Num(3))))` -> `5`

```
uv run dojo test csharp v3ca02_pattern_matching ex7
```

