## Exercise 7 — a static abstract member of your own

Edit `src/Lessons/V3CA03_GenericsConstraints/Ex7.cs`.

```csharp
public interface IFromRow<TSelf> where TSelf : IFromRow<TSelf>
{
    static abstract TSelf FromRow(string[] cells);
    static abstract int ExpectedColumns { get; }
}

public sealed record User(int Id, string Name) : IFromRow<User>;
public sealed record Reading(string Sensor, double Value) : IFromRow<Reading>;

public static class RowReader
{
    public static List<T> ReadAll<T>(IEnumerable<string[]> rows) where T : IFromRow<T>;
    public static int Skipped { get; }
}
```

`ReadAll` materialises every row whose length equals `T.ExpectedColumns`, and
counts the rest in `Skipped` (reset at the start of each call). No reflection,
no `Activator`, no factory delegate parameter — the type argument carries the
construction.

- `User.FromRow(["7", "ada"])` -> `new User(7, "ada")`, `User.ExpectedColumns` -> `2`
- `Reading.FromRow(["s3", "20.5"])` -> `new Reading("s3", 20.5)`, parsed with
  `CultureInfo.InvariantCulture`
- `ReadAll<User>([["1","a"], ["oops"], ["2","b"]])` -> two users, `Skipped` is `1`

The `where TSelf : IFromRow<TSelf>` constraint on the interface is not
decoration. Delete it and read the error at the `T.FromRow` call site — record
the code in a comment.

