## Challenge — `Result<TValue, TError>`

Edit `src/Lessons/V3CA03_GenericsConstraints/Challenge.cs`.

```csharp
public readonly struct Result<TValue, TError>
{
    public static Result<TValue, TError> Ok(TValue value);
    public static Result<TValue, TError> Fail(TError error);

    public bool IsOk { get; }

    public Result<TNext, TError> Map<TNext>(Func<TValue, TNext> project);
    public Result<TNext, TError> Bind<TNext>(Func<TValue, Result<TNext, TError>> project);
    public TOut Match<TOut>(Func<TValue, TOut> onOk, Func<TError, TOut> onError);
}

public static class Results
{
    public static Result<T, TError> SumOf<T, TError>(IEnumerable<Result<T, TError>> results)
        where T : INumber<T>;
}
```

`Map` transforms the value and leaves an error untouched. `Bind` is the same
but the projection itself returns a `Result`, so it flattens — the difference
that stops you accumulating `Result<Result<T, E>, E>`.

`SumOf` folds a sequence, starting at `T.Zero`, and **short-circuits on the
first error**: the gate passes a sequence whose later elements would throw if
evaluated, and asserts they never were.

Worked examples:

- `Result<int, string>.Ok(2).Map(v => v * 3).Match(v => $"ok {v}", e => e)` -> `"ok 6"`
- `Result<int, string>.Fail("bad").Map(v => v * 3).Match(v => $"ok {v}", e => e)` -> `"bad"`
- `Ok(4).Bind(v => v > 0 ? Ok(v) : Fail("neg"))` -> ok with `4`
- `SumOf([Ok(1), Ok(2), Ok(3)])` -> ok with `6`
- `SumOf([Ok(1), Fail("boom"), Ok(3)])` -> fail with `"boom"`

Constraints: `Result` is a `readonly struct` and must not allocate on the
success path; `SumOf` enumerates the sequence at most once; and `Match` is the
only way to read the value out, so there is no `Value` property to misuse.

```
uv run dojo test csharp v3ca03_generics_constraints challenge
```

