## Review 1 — when null is the right answer

Edit `src/Lessons/V3CA04_NullableReferences/Review1.cs`.

```csharp
public sealed class Node
{
    public Node(string value, Node? next = null);
    public string Value { get; }
    public Node? Next { get; set; }
}

public static class Chain
{
    public static int Length(Node? head);
    public static string Join(Node? head, string separator);
    public static Node? Last(Node? head);
    public static bool TryFind(Node? head, string value, [NotNullWhen(true)] out Node? found);
}
```

Every `?` above is deliberate: the end of the chain *is* null, and annotating it
away would be dishonest. Implement the four methods with no `!` and no
warnings.

- `Length(null)` -> `0`; `Length(a -> b -> c)` -> `3`
- `Join(null, ", ")` -> `""`; `Join(a -> b, "-")` -> `"a-b"`
- `Last(null)` -> `null`; `Last(a -> b)` -> the `b` node
- `TryFind(a -> b, "b", out Node? found)` -> `true`, and `found.Value`
  compiles with no null check
- `TryFind(a -> b, "z", out Node? found)` -> `false`

Then write a short comment answering: this sector spent eight exercises
removing nulls, so why is `Next` nullable rather than, say, a sentinel
end-of-chain node?

```
uv run dojo test csharp v3ca04_nullable_reference_types review1
```

